1 @
 Runs a command procedure: a file of DCL commands, each line starting
 with a dollar sign. The procedure runs at a new procedure level, with
 its own local symbols, its own ON settings and the parameters P1 to P8.

 Format

   @file-spec [p1 [p2 [... p8]]]
2 Parameters
file-spec

 The procedure to run. The default type is .COM, and the rest of the
 spec comes from the current default directory.

p1 to p8

 Up to eight parameters, separated by blanks. DCL upcases them unless
 they are in quotation marks, and takes the quotation marks away. The
 procedure sees them as the local symbols P1 to P8; those not given are
 empty strings.
2 Qualifiers
/OUTPUT

    /OUTPUT=file-spec

 Sends the procedure's SYS$OUTPUT, and what the images it runs write,
 to a new version of file-spec instead of the terminal. DCL's messages
 go to the file, and to SYS$ERROR as well.
2 Examples
 1.$ @BUILD

   Runs BUILD.COM from the current default directory.

 2.$ @[.TOOLS]COPYALL "My File" B

   P1 is "My File" as typed, P2 is "B".

 3.$ @NIGHTLY/OUTPUT=NIGHTLY.LOG

   Runs NIGHTLY.COM with its output in NIGHTLY.LOG.
2 Nesting
 Procedures may call procedures, and CALL starts a level too; vmsport,
 like VMS, stops at 32 levels with %DCL-W-STKOVF.
1 ANALYZE
 vmsport's ANALYZE examines RMS files only (/RMS_FILE): it checks a
 file's structure and reports what it finds, or describes the file in
 FDL, ready for CREATE/FDL.

 Format

   ANALYZE/RMS_FILE file-spec[,...]
2 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 The files to analyze; wildcards are allowed. /FDL describes only the
 first.
2 Qualifiers
/RMS_FILE

 Required: the files are RMS files.
/CHECK

    /CHECK (default)

 Reports the file's header, attributes and structure, and the errors
 found, then the command. Without /OUTPUT the reports go to SYS$OUTPUT.
/FDL

 Writes an FDL description of the file instead, to name.FDL in the
 current directory unless /OUTPUT names another file.
/OUTPUT

    /OUTPUT=file-spec

 Where the reports go (default type .ANL, or .FDL with /FDL). With
 /OUTPUT, each file analyzed is also reported as %ANLRMS-I-ERRORS with
 its count of errors.
1 ASSIGN
 Defines a logical name, like DEFINE, with the two names the other way
 round: the equivalence first, then the logical name.

 Format

   ASSIGN equivalence-name[,...] logical-name[:]
2 Parameters
equivalence-name[,...]

 The value (or, separated by commas, the values of a search list) the
 logical name stands for. Quotation marks keep case and blanks.

logical-name

 The name to define. A trailing colon is ignored.
2 Qualifiers
/TABLE

    /TABLE=name

 The logical name table to define it in. The default is LNM$PROCESS.
/PROCESS
 Defines the name in the process table (the default).
/JOB
 Defines the name in the job table, shared by the processes of your
 job (in vmsport, the processes of one Unix login session).
/GROUP
 Defines the name in the group table, shared by your Unix group.
/SYSTEM
 Defines the name in the system table. vmsportd keeps one per user.
/TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES

    /TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES=(keyword[,...])

 CONCEALED: when the name is a device, file specs show the logical name,
 not its equivalence. TERMINAL: the equivalence is not translated any
 further.
/LOG

    /LOG (default)
    /NOLOG

 When the name already had a value, says so with %DCL-I-SUPERSEDE.
/USER_MODE
 Accepted. vmsport defines names in supervisor mode whatever is asked;
 access modes are kept as data and protect nothing.
/SUPERVISOR_MODE
 The mode vmsport always uses.
/EXECUTIVE_MODE
 Accepted, see /USER_MODE.
/KERNEL_MODE
 Accepted, see /USER_MODE.
2 Example
 $ ASSIGN HOST:[Users.me.data.] DATA

   Same as DEFINE DATA HOST:[Users.me.data.].
1 CALL
 Calls a subroutine of the current procedure: a block that starts with
 label: SUBROUTINE and ends with ENDSUBROUTINE. The subroutine runs at a
 new procedure level, with its own local symbols, ON settings and
 parameters P1 to P8.

 Format

   CALL label [p1 [p2 [... p8]]]
2 Parameters
label

 The label of the SUBROUTINE line.

p1 to p8

 As for @: separated by blanks, upcased unless quoted.
2 Status
 EXIT [status] leaves the subroutine with that status; reaching
 ENDSUBROUTINE leaves it with %X00030001. The status is in $STATUS
 afterwards, and a failure the subroutine didn't show is shown.
2 Example
 $ CALL SQUARE 7
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT RESULT
 $ EXIT
 $SQUARE: SUBROUTINE
 $ RESULT == P1 * P1
 $ ENDSUBROUTINE
1 CLOSE
 Closes a file opened with OPEN.

 Format

   CLOSE logical-name
2 Parameters
logical-name

 The name given to OPEN. A name that isn't open gives
 %DCL-W-UNDFIL.
2 Qualifiers
/ERROR

    /ERROR=label

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't take it yet.
/DISPOSITION

    /DISPOSITION=keyword

 Accepted; the file is always kept.
1 Command_Procedures
 A command procedure is a file of DCL commands that @ runs. A line that
 starts with $ is a command; other lines are data and are skipped. A
 command continues on the next line when it ends with a hyphen. An
 exclamation mark starts a comment, outside quotation marks.

 A line may start with a label, "name:", for GOTO, GOSUB and CALL.
2 Levels
 Each procedure, and each CALL, is a level with its own local symbols,
 its own P1 to P8 and its own ON settings. Locals of outer levels are
 visible; globals (==) are visible everywhere. F$ENVIRONMENT("DEPTH")
 tells the level, 0 at the terminal.
2 Status
 Every command leaves its status in $STATUS, a string like "%X00030001",
 and its severity in $SEVERITY (0 warning, 1 success, 2 error,
 3 informational, 4 severe). Commands DCL does itself leave %X00030001
 when they work; WRITE and READ leave %X00010001.

 A procedure that exits with a failure shows its message once, in the
 caller, unless it was shown already.
2 Errors
 By default a procedure exits when a command fails with an error or a
 severe error (ON ERROR THEN EXIT). ON chooses another action and another
 severity; SET NOON turns this off for the level, SET ON back on.
2 Verify
 SET VERIFY echoes each line before it runs, as it is in the file after
 apostrophe substitution; continuation lines show separately.
2 LOGIN.COM
 An interactive dcl session runs SYS$LOGIN:LOGIN.COM first, if there is
 one. dcl -c and dcl FILE.COM don't.
1 CONTINUE
 Does nothing, and leaves $STATUS as it was. It is mostly an ON action:
 ON WARNING THEN CONTINUE goes on after warnings.

 Format

   CONTINUE
1 COPY
 Copies files, record by record, keeping their record attributes
 (record format and carriage control, in the vms.fab extended
 attribute). Each copy is a new version of the output.

 Format

   COPY input-file-spec[,...] output-file-spec
2 Parameters
input-file-spec[,...]

 The files to copy; wildcards allowed. A plus sign between files
 concatenates them, as a comma does when the output is one file.

output-file-spec

 Where to copy to. Fields it leaves out, and wildcards in it, come from
 each input: COPY *.TXT *.BAK copies every .TXT to a .BAK. When several
 inputs go to one output without wildcards, they are concatenated into
 it.
2 Qualifiers
/LOG
 Shows each copy, %COPY-S-COPIED, or each file added to a
 concatenation, %COPY-S-APPENDED.
/CONCATENATE
 Accepted. vmsport decides by itself: several inputs and an output with
 no wildcard are concatenated.
/EXCLUDE

    /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't skip anything yet.
2 Examples
 1.$ COPY NOTES.TXT [.BACKUP]/LOG

   Copies the newest NOTES.TXT to [.BACKUP]NOTES.TXT, a new version.

 2.$ COPY A.TXT,B.TXT ALL.TXT

   Concatenates A.TXT and B.TXT into a new ALL.TXT.
1 CREATE
 Makes files: a sequential file of the records typed after the command
 (in a command procedure, its data lines), new directories
 (/DIRECTORY), or an empty file of any organization as an FDL file
 describes it (/FDL).

 Format

   CREATE file-spec[,...]
   CREATE/DIRECTORY directory-spec[,...]
   CREATE/FDL=fdl-file-spec [file-spec]
2 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 The files to create, each a new version. Only the first gets the
 records; with /FDL the FDL's NAME is used when no file is given.
2 Qualifiers
/DIRECTORY

 Creates the directories named, and those above them that aren't there
 yet. One that exists already is reported as %CREATE-I-EXISTS.
/FDL

    /FDL=fdl-file-spec

 Creates an empty sequential, relative or indexed file with the
 attributes the FDL gives (default type .FDL; SYS$INPUT reads it from
 the data lines that follow). Statements it doesn't understand are
 reported by number, and no file is made.
/LOG

 Reports each file or directory created.
1 DEASSIGN
 Removes a logical name.

 Format

   DEASSIGN [logical-name[:]]
2 Parameters
logical-name

 The name to remove. A name that isn't there gives
 %SYSTEM-F-NOLOGNAM. Leave it out with /ALL.
2 Qualifiers
/TABLE

    /TABLE=name

 The table to remove it from. The default is LNM$PROCESS.
/PROCESS
 The process table (the default).
/JOB
 The job table.
/GROUP
 The group table.
/SYSTEM
 The system table.
/ALL
 Removes every name in the table.
/USER_MODE
 Accepted; vmsport deassigns at supervisor mode.
/SUPERVISOR_MODE
 The mode vmsport always uses.
/EXECUTIVE_MODE
 Accepted, see /USER_MODE.
/KERNEL_MODE
 Accepted, see /USER_MODE.
1 DEFINE
 Defines a logical name: a name that stands for one or more values,
 mostly file specs or parts of them. See also Logical_Names.

 Format

   DEFINE logical-name equivalence-name[,...]
2 Parameters
logical-name

 The name to define. DCL upcases it.

equivalence-name[,...]

 Its value, or the values of a search list. DCL upcases them unless they
 are in quotation marks; quotation marks keep case and blanks and are not
 part of the value.
2 Qualifiers
/TABLE

    /TABLE=name

 The logical name table to define it in. The default is LNM$PROCESS.
/PROCESS
 Defines the name in the process table (the default). Images DCL runs
 get the process table, and the names they define come back to it.
/JOB
 Defines the name in the job table: the processes of one Unix login
 session share it, and vmsportd keeps it.
/GROUP
 Defines the name in the group table, shared by your Unix group.
/SYSTEM
 Defines the name in the system table. vmsportd keeps one per user, so
 "system" means all of your processes.
/TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES

    /TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES=(keyword[,...])

 CONCEALED: a device name that file specs show instead of its
 equivalence. TERMINAL: the equivalence isn't translated further. A
 concealed name whose value ends in ".]" is a rooted device:
 DEFINE/TRANSLATION=CONCEALED PROJ HOST:[Users.me.proj.] makes
 PROJ:[SRC] the host directory /Users/me/proj/SRC.
/LOG

    /LOG (default)
    /NOLOG

 When the name already had a value, says so with %DCL-I-SUPERSEDE.
/USER_MODE
 Accepted. vmsport defines names in supervisor mode whatever is asked;
 access modes are kept but protect nothing.
/SUPERVISOR_MODE
 The mode vmsport always uses.
/EXECUTIVE_MODE
 Accepted, see /USER_MODE.
/KERNEL_MODE
 Accepted, see /USER_MODE.
2 Examples
 1.$ DEFINE NOTES SYS$LOGIN:NOTES.TXT
   $ TYPE NOTES

   A bare name is tried as a logical name for a file.

 2.$ DEFINE/TRANSLATION=CONCEALED WORK HOST:[Users.me.work.]
   $ SET DEFAULT WORK:[000000]

   A device of your own over a host directory.

 3.$ DEFINE TOOLS SYS$LOGIN:[TOOLS], SYS$LOGIN:[OLDTOOLS]

   A search list: files are looked for in both, in that order.
1 DELETE
 Deletes files, or with /SYMBOL symbols. Deleting a file deletes one
 version of it: on the host, the file NAME.TYP;n (or NAME.TYP for
 version 1 of a plain Unix file).

 Format

   DELETE file-spec[,...]
   DELETE/SYMBOL [symbol-name]
2 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 The files to delete. Each must give a version, as on VMS: ;n, ;0 (the
 newest), ;-1 (the one before) or ;* (all). Wildcards allowed. Without a
 version DELETE fails with %DELETE-W-DELVER.
2 Qualifiers
/LOG
 Shows each file deleted, %DELETE-I-FILDEL, and the total.
/CONFIRM
 Accepted; vmsport doesn't ask yet.
/SYMBOL
 Deletes a symbol instead of files: see DELETE /SYMBOL.
/BEFORE

    /BEFORE=time

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't select by date yet.
/SINCE

    /SINCE=time

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't select by date yet.
/EXCLUDE

    /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't skip anything yet.
2 /SYMBOL
 Deletes a symbol. DCL does this itself.

 Format

   DELETE/SYMBOL [symbol-name]
3 Parameters
symbol-name

 The symbol to delete; an abbreviation defined with * deletes the whole
 name. A symbol that isn't there gives %DCL-W-UNDSYM.
3 Qualifiers
/SYMBOL
 Says this is DELETE/SYMBOL.
/LOCAL
 Deletes from the local symbols of the current level (the default).
/GLOBAL
 Deletes from the global symbols.
/ALL
 Deletes every symbol of the level, or with /GLOBAL every global one.
/LOG
 Accepted; nothing is shown.
2 Examples
 1.$ DELETE *.TMP;*

   Deletes every version of every .TMP file.

 2.$ DELETE/LOG REPORT.TXT;-1

   Deletes the version before the newest.

 3.$ DELETE/SYMBOL/GLOBAL COUNT
1 DIRECTORY
 Lists files, laid out as VMS does: a heading per directory, the names
 in columns, and a trailing total. Directories show as NAME.DIR;1, and a
 plain Unix file with no ;n in its name is version 1.

 Format

   DIRECTORY [file-spec[,...]]
2 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 What to list; the default is *.*;* in the current default directory.
 Wildcards are allowed in names, types, versions and directories:
 [...] is a directory and all those below it, [*] the ones just below.
2 Qualifiers
/BRIEF
 Names only (the default).
/COLUMNS

    /COLUMNS=n

 Lists in n columns. With /SIZE or /DATE, names that don't fit are cut
 and end in a vertical bar.
/DATE

    /DATE[=keyword]

 Shows each file's date, from the host file system. The keywords
 CREATED (the default), MODIFIED and ALL are accepted.
/FULL
 Shows what vmsport knows of each file: sizes, dates, record attributes,
 and the owner and protection as the host has them.
/GRAND_TOTAL
 Shows only the totals for all the directories listed.
/HEADING

    /HEADING (default)
    /NOHEADING

 /NOHEADING leaves out the "Directory ..." lines and shows each file's
 full spec.
/OWNER
 Accepted; see /FULL for the owner.
/PROTECTION
 Accepted; see /FULL for the protection.
/SIZE

    /SIZE[=keyword]

 Shows each file's size in 512-byte blocks: USED (the default), the
 host's ALLOCATION, or ALL (both).
/TOTAL
 Shows only each directory's totals.
/TRAILING

    /TRAILING (default)
    /NOTRAILING

 /NOTRAILING leaves out the "Total of ..." lines.
/VERSIONS

    /VERSIONS=n

 Lists at most the n newest versions of each file.
/OUTPUT

    /OUTPUT=file-spec

 Accepted; the listing goes to SYS$OUTPUT.
/EXCLUDE

    /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't skip anything yet.
2 Examples
 1.$ DIRECTORY/SIZE

 2.$ DIRECTORY [...]*.COM

   Every .COM file here and below.

 3.$ DIRECTORY/VERSIONS=1 SYS$LOGIN:

   The newest version of each file in your home directory.
1 DISMOUNT
 Ends the use of a device MOUNT made from a Files-11 image file. The
 files that changed, came or went while it was mounted are written back
 into the image, and the logical name MOUNT defined goes away.

 Format

   DISMOUNT device-name[:]
2 Parameters
device-name[:]

 The device, as MOUNT named it (DKA100:).
1 ELSE
 Starts the part of an IF block that runs when the condition is false.
 See IF.

 Format

   ELSE
1 ENDIF
 Ends an IF block. See IF.

 Format

   ENDIF
1 ENDSUBROUTINE
 Ends a SUBROUTINE block. See SUBROUTINE.

 Format

   ENDSUBROUTINE
1 EOJ
 Ends the session, like LOGOUT.

 Format

   EOJ
1 EXIT
 Leaves the current procedure level (a procedure or a CALL) and goes back
 to the one that started it.

 Format

   EXIT [status]
2 Parameters
status

 An expression for the status to leave with; $STATUS has it afterwards.
 Without it the level leaves with the status of the last command. A
 failure is shown, unless it was shown already. At the terminal EXIT
 doesn't leave dcl, but shows a failing status.
2 Example
 $ IF F$SEARCH(P1) .EQS. "" THEN EXIT %X10018292

   Leaves with RMS-E-FNF, marked as shown (bit 28), so no message.
1 Expressions
 DCL's values are 32-bit integers and strings. An expression is used
 after = and ==, in IF, WRITE, EXIT, RETURN, and as the arguments of
 lexical functions.
2 Operators
 From the lowest precedence to the highest:

   .OR.                       bitwise or
   .AND.                      bitwise and
   .NOT.                      bitwise complement
   .EQ. .NE. .LT. .LE. .GT. .GE.     compare as integers
   .EQS. .NES. .LTS. .LES. .GTS. .GES.  compare as strings
   + -                        add, subtract (see Strings)
   * /                        multiply, divide
   unary + -

 Parentheses group. Comparisons give 1 (true) or 0. Arithmetic wraps
 at 32 bits; division by zero gives 0 and no error.
2 Values
 Integers are written in decimal, or with a radix: %X1F (hex), %O17
 (octal), %D99 (decimal). Strings are in quotation marks; a doubled
 quotation mark is one quotation mark. A string runs to the end of the
 line if it isn't closed. A symbol name stands for its value; an
 undefined one gives %DCL-W-UNDSYM.
2 Strings
 String + string is the two joined. String - string is the first with
 the first occurrence of the second taken out. When one side is an
 integer, the string is turned into an integer.

 A string as an integer: its number if it is one (radix forms too),
 otherwise 1 if it starts with T, t, Y or y, otherwise 0. An integer as
 a string: its decimal digits.
2 Truth
 A value is true when its integer value is odd: 1, "YES", "TRUE" and "1"
 are true; 0, 2, "NO" and "" are false.
1 Foreign_Commands
 A foreign command is a symbol whose value starts with a dollar sign: the
 rest names a program, which runs with the words of the command line as
 its Unix argv, as typed.

 Format

   symbol :== $program [arguments]
2 Program
 The program is a VMS file spec (SYS$LOGIN:[BIN]TOOL, type .EXE, or a
 host program with no type) or a Unix path. := and :== upcase what isn't
 quoted, so $/bin/ls becomes $/BIN/LS; vmsport finds Unix paths whatever
 their case. A vmsport program can also get the line as LIB$GET_FOREIGN
 returns it: upcased and squeezed outside quotation marks.
2 DCL$PATH
 A verb DCL doesn't know is looked for in the directories DCL$PATH names
 (a logical name, possibly a search list): VERB.COM runs as a procedure,
 else VERB.EXE or an executable file named verb (lowercase) runs as a
 foreign command.

 $ DEFINE DCL$PATH HOST:[usr.bin], HOST:[usr.local.bin]
 $ wc -l NOTES.TXT
2 Status
 A program that exits with Unix code 0 leaves SS$_NORMAL in $STATUS; any
 other code n leaves %X1035A002 + n*8, an error marked as shown. vmsport
 programs send their full 32-bit status instead.
2 Example
 $ LS :== "$/bin/ls"
 $ LS -l

   With quotation marks the path keeps its case.
1 GOSUB
 Goes to a label in the current procedure and comes back with RETURN.
 GOSUBs nest; they don't start a procedure level, so symbols are shared.

 Format

   GOSUB label
2 Parameters
label

 The label to go to. One that isn't there gives %DCL-W-USGOSUB.
2 Example
 $ GOSUB SHOW_IT
 $ EXIT
 $SHOW_IT:
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "here"
 $ RETURN
1 GOTO
 Goes to a label in the current procedure. Going out of an IF block ends
 the block.

 Format

   GOTO label
2 Parameters
label

 The label: a line that starts with "label:". One that isn't there gives
 %DCL-W-USGOTO.
1 HELP
 Shows help from a help library: a topic, its text, and the subtopics it
 has. Run at a terminal, HELP then asks for a subtopic: type one to see
 it, ? to see the current topic again, Return to go up a level, and
 Ctrl/Z to leave.

 vmsport reads help libraries as .HLP text (keys "1 TOPIC", "2 SUBTOPIC",
 and /QUALIFIER entries under Qualifiers), where VMS uses .HLB files made
 by LIBRARY/HELP.

 Format

   HELP [topic [subtopic [...]]]
2 Parameters
topic

 What to show. Abbreviations work; * and % are wildcards; "topic..."
 shows the topic and everything under it. A qualifier written after a
 verb, as in HELP DIRECTORY/SIZE, is that verb's qualifier.
2 Qualifiers
/LIBRARY

    /LIBRARY=file-spec

 The help library to use instead of SYS$HELP:HELPLIB.HLP.
/PROMPT

    /PROMPT (default)
    /NOPROMPT

 /NOPROMPT shows the help and stops, without asking for more. HELP also
 doesn't ask when its input isn't a terminal.
/INSTRUCTIONS

    /INSTRUCTIONS (default)
    /NOINSTRUCTIONS

 HELP with no topic starts with a few lines on how to use it;
 /NOINSTRUCTIONS leaves them out.
/OUTPUT

    /OUTPUT=file-spec

 Writes the help to a file instead of SYS$OUTPUT.
/PAGE
 Stops after each screenful.
/USERLIBRARY

    /USERLIBRARY=(table[,...])

 Where HELP also looks for help libraries of your own, named by the
 logical names HLP$LIBRARY, HLP$LIBRARY_1, and so on: the PROCESS, JOB,
 GROUP and SYSTEM tables, ALL of them, or NONE.
2 /HELP
 In vmsport every command takes /HELP: instead of running, it shows its
 help, as HELP verb would. SET DEFAULT/HELP shows HELP SET DEFAULT. The
 utilities run from a Unix shell take it too: directory /help. A verb
 with no help of its own shows what its command table says it takes.
2 Examples
 1.$ HELP COPY

 2.$ HELP SHOW LOGICAL/FULL

 3.$ HELP/NOPROMPT Lexicals F$PARSE
1 IF
 Runs a command, or a block of commands, when an expression is true.

 Format

   IF expression THEN [$] command

   IF expression
   THEN
       commands
   [ELSE
       commands]
   ENDIF
2 Parameters
expression

 Any expression; it is true when its integer value is odd (see
 Expressions, Truth).

command

 On the same line as THEN, the command that runs when the expression is
 true. Nothing after THEN starts a block.
2 Blocks
 THEN, ELSE and ENDIF of a block are commands of their own, each on its
 own line. Blocks nest. An IF without THEN on its line wants THEN as the
 next command. GOTO out of a block ends it.
2 THEN
 Starts the commands that run when the IF's expression is true.
2 ELSE
 Starts the commands that run when it is false.
2 ENDIF
 Ends the block.
2 Example
 $ IF F$SEARCH("LOGIN.COM") .NES. ""
 $ THEN
 $     WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "found"
 $ ELSE
 $     WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "not found"
 $ ENDIF
1 INQUIRE
 Asks a question at the terminal and puts the answer in a symbol.

 Format

   INQUIRE symbol-name [prompt-string]
2 Parameters
symbol-name

 The symbol for the answer. DCL upcases the answer and squeezes its
 blanks, unless it is in quotation marks.

prompt-string

 The question; the default is the symbol's name.
2 Qualifiers
/LOCAL
 Defines a local symbol (the default).
/GLOBAL
 Defines a global symbol.
/PUNCTUATION

    /PUNCTUATION (default)
    /NOPUNCTUATION

 Puts ": " after the prompt.
1 Lexicals
 Lexical functions return facts about strings, symbols, files, logical
 names, times and the process. They are written F$NAME(arguments) in
 expressions, and inside apostrophes ('F$LENGTH(X)') in command lines.
 An argument may be left empty, as in F$PARSE(X,,,"NAME").

 Not there yet: F$FILE_ATTRIBUTES, F$GETDVI, F$CONTEXT, F$IDENTIFIER,
 F$PRIVILEGE, F$CSID, F$DELTA_TIME and F$READLINE; they give
 %DCL-W-IVFNAM.
2 F$CVSI
 F$CVSI(start-bit, bits, string): the bits of string, from start-bit
 (counting from the low bit of the first character), as a signed
 integer.
2 F$CVTIME
 F$CVTIME([time [, format [, field]]]): converts a time.

 time: an absolute time (dd-MMM-yyyy hh:mm:ss.cc, parts may be left
 out), a delta time (dddd-hh:mm:ss.cc), a combination (time+delta,
 time-delta), or TODAY, TOMORROW, YESTERDAY. The default is now.

 format: ABSOLUTE, COMPARISON (yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss.cc, the default) or
 DELTA.

 field: DATE, DATETIME, DAY, DAYOFYEAR, HOUR, HUNDREDTH, MINUTE, MONTH,
 SECOND, TIME, WEEKDAY, YEAR and the like. Without it the whole time.
2 F$CVUI
 F$CVUI(start-bit, bits, string): as F$CVSI, unsigned.
2 F$DIRECTORY
 F$DIRECTORY(): the current default directory, [dir] only.
2 F$EDIT
 F$EDIT(string, edits): string, edited. edits is a list of COLLAPSE
 (drop blanks and tabs), COMPRESS (runs of them become one blank),
 LOWERCASE, TRIM (drop them at both ends), UNCOMMENT (drop from the first
 ! on) and UPCASE. Text in quotation marks is left alone, except by TRIM
 and UNCOMMENT.
2 F$ELEMENT
 F$ELEMENT(n, delimiter, string): the n-th element (from 0) of string,
 split at delimiter, a single character. Past the last element it
 returns the delimiter.
2 F$ENVIRONMENT
 F$ENVIRONMENT(item): about the DCL session. Items: DEPTH (procedure
 level, 0 at the terminal), PROCEDURE (the running procedure's spec),
 DEFAULT (default directory), ON_SEVERITY (WARNING, ERROR, SEVERE_ERROR
 or NONE), VERIFY_PROCEDURE and VERIFY_IMAGE, MESSAGE (the SET MESSAGE
 flags), INTERACTIVE, CAPTIVE, MAX_DEPTH, PROMPT, ON_CONTROL_Y,
 SYMBOL_SCOPE.
2 F$EXTRACT
 F$EXTRACT(start, length, string): length characters of string from
 start (from 0). Past the end it gives what there is; a negative start
 or length gives "".
2 F$FAO
 F$FAO(control-string [, argument ...]): formats with $FAO directives:
 !AS a string, !UL !SL !XL !OL !ZL a number (also B and W sizes),
 !n<...!> a field, !%S a plural s, !%D and !%T a time, !/ a new line,
 !_ a tab, !! an exclamation mark, a width in front (!10AS) and more.
2 F$GETJPI
 F$GETJPI(pid, item): about this process. vmsport answers USERNAME, PID,
 MASTER_PID, UIC, GRP, MEM, PRCNAM, MODE, NODENAME and VERSION; the pid
 is ignored.
2 F$GETSYI
 F$GETSYI(item): about the system: NODENAME (the host name), SCSNODE,
 VERSION and ARCH_NAME.
2 F$INTEGER
 F$INTEGER(expression): the value as an integer (see Expressions,
 Strings).
2 F$LENGTH
 F$LENGTH(string): its length.
2 F$LOCATE
 F$LOCATE(substring, string): where substring starts in string (from 0),
 or the length of string if it isn't there.
2 F$LOGICAL
 F$LOGICAL(name): the old form of F$TRNLNM(name).
2 F$MATCH_WILD
 F$MATCH_WILD(candidate, pattern): "TRUE" if candidate matches pattern,
 with * and % wildcards, ignoring case; else "FALSE".
2 F$MESSAGE
 F$MESSAGE(status [, components]): the message for a status, from the
 system messages and SET MESSAGE files. components lists the parts to
 return: FACILITY, SEVERITY, IDENT, TEXT (the default is all of them).
2 F$MODE
 F$MODE(): "INTERACTIVE".
2 F$PARSE
 F$PARSE(file-spec [, default-spec [, related-spec [, field [, type]]]]):
 file-spec with what it leaves out taken from the other two specs, then
 the default device and directory, after logical names: the full spec,
 upcased, or one field, NODE, DEVICE, DIRECTORY, NAME, TYPE or VERSION.

 The device must exist, and for the full spec the directory too;
 otherwise F$PARSE returns "". type SYNTAX_ONLY checks the syntax only.
 A spec that is just a name may be a logical name for a file.
2 F$PID
 F$PID(context): the process id, in hex.
2 F$SEARCH
 F$SEARCH(file-spec [, stream]): the next file matching file-spec, a
 full spec, or "" when there are no more. Call it again with the same
 spec for the next one; a different spec starts again. stream numbers
 keep several searches apart.
2 F$STRING
 F$STRING(expression): the value as a string.
2 F$TIME
 F$TIME(): the date and time now, dd-MMM-yyyy hh:mm:ss.cc.
2 F$TRNLNM
 F$TRNLNM(name [, table [, index [, mode [, case [, item]]]]]): a logical
 name's value, or "". table defaults to LNM$DCL_LOGICAL (process, job,
 group, system); index picks a value of a search list (from 0); names are
 matched case-blind; mode and case are taken but not used. item is VALUE
 (the default), LENGTH, MAX_INDEX, TABLE_NAME, TABLE, TERMINAL,
 CONCEALED, CONFINE, NO_ALIAS, CRELOG or ACCESS_MODE.
2 F$TYPE
 F$TYPE(symbol-name): "INTEGER" if the symbol is an integer or a string
 that reads as one, "STRING" for other strings, "" if there is no such
 symbol. The argument must be a symbol name.
2 F$UNIQUE
 F$UNIQUE(): a string different each time.
2 F$USER
 F$USER(): the UIC, [group,member] in octal from the Unix gid and uid.
2 F$VERIFY
 F$VERIFY([procedure-value [, image-value]]): 1 if SET VERIFY is on, else
 0; with an argument, turns it on (true) or off (false) as well.
1 Logical_Names
 A logical name is a name that stands for one or more values: usually a
 device, a directory or a whole file spec. File specs are translated
 through logical names, up to 10 levels, until a value is TERMINAL or
 isn't a logical name.
2 Tables
 LNM$PROCESS     this process; images DCL runs get a copy, and what
                 they change comes back
 LNM$JOB         the processes of one job: a Unix login session
 LNM$GROUP       your Unix group
 LNM$SYSTEM      in vmsport, all of your processes

 vmsportd, a per-user daemon started on first use, keeps the job, group
 and system tables. LNM$FILE_DEV, used for file specs, and
 LNM$DCL_LOGICAL, used by SHOW LOGICAL and F$TRNLNM, are search lists of
 these four.
2 Search_Lists
 A name with several values is a search list: DEFINE X A:, B:. Opening a
 file through it tries each value in turn; DIRECTORY and F$SEARCH look
 in all of them.
2 Devices
 HOST: is the host's / and the only real device. Everything else is a
 logical name over it. A concealed name that ends in ".]" is a rooted
 device: its directory is the top, [000000], of the device.

 SYS$LOGIN        your home directory
 SYS$SCRATCH      the same
 SYS$SYSROOT      vmsport's sys directory, rooted and concealed
 SYS$SYSTEM       SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSEXE]
 SYS$MESSAGE      SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSMSG]
 SYS$LIBRARY      SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSLIB] (command tables)
 SYS$HELP         SYS$SYSROOT:[SYSHLP] (help libraries)
 SYS$DISK         the default device
2 From_Unix
 The lnm tool shows and sets job logical names from a Unix shell:
 lnm NAME, lnm NAME=VALUE[,VALUE], lnm -d NAME.
1 LOGOUT
 Ends the DCL session. In a subprocess made by SPAWN it says "Process
 name logged out at ..." and control goes back to the parent.

 Format

   LOGOUT
2 Qualifiers
/BRIEF
 Accepted; no logout message is shown.
/FULL
 Accepted; no accounting is shown.
1 MOUNT
 Makes a Files-11 disk image (ODS-2 or ODS-5, as INITIALIZE or the ods
 tool make it, or as copied from a VMS system) a device, so its files can
 be used as DKA100:[DIR]FILE.TYP. Every process of the user sees it until
 DISMOUNT, which writes the changes back into the image.

 vmsport names the image first, where VMS names a drive.

 Format

   MOUNT image-file device-name[:] [volume-label] [logical-name[:]]
2 Parameters
image-file

 The host file holding the volume.
device-name[:]

 The name the volume gets: DKA100:, LDA1: or any other.
volume-label

 The label the volume must have; MOUNT fails with INCVOLLABEL when it
 has another.
logical-name[:]

 The logical name to define for the device in the job table; by default
 DISK$label.
2 Qualifiers
/OVERRIDE

    /OVERRIDE=IDENTIFICATION

 Mounts the volume whatever its label.
/WRITE

    /WRITE (default)
    /NOWRITE

 With /NOWRITE, DISMOUNT leaves the image as it was, whatever was
 changed on the device.
1 ON
 Says what to do when a command in this procedure level fails.

 Format

   ON condition THEN command
2 Parameters
condition

 WARNING (warnings and worse), ERROR (errors and severe errors; the
 default, with EXIT as the command) or SEVERE_ERROR. CONTROL_Y is
 accepted and does nothing yet.

command

 What to run: often GOTO label, or CONTINUE to go on. It runs once; then
 the level is back to ON ERROR THEN EXIT.
2 Notes
 SET NOON turns the checks off for the level, SET ON back on. Each level
 (procedure or CALL) has its own ON setting.
2 Example
 $ ON ERROR THEN GOTO CLEANUP
1 OPEN
 Opens a sequential file for READ and WRITE, under a logical name.

 Format

   OPEN logical-name file-spec
2 Parameters
logical-name

 The name READ, WRITE and CLOSE use for the file.

file-spec

 The file. If it can't be opened, DCL shows %DCL-E-OPENIN (or OPENOUT)
 with the reason, and $STATUS has the RMS status.
2 Qualifiers
/READ
 Opens it for reading (the default).
/WRITE
 Makes a new version and opens it for writing. vmsport writes stream_LF
 text files (VMS makes VFC ones). /READ/WRITE together opens for reading
 only for now.
/APPEND
 Opens the newest version and writes after its end.
/SHARE

    /SHARE[=READ|WRITE]

 Lets others open the file too: READ lets them read it, WRITE (the
 default) read and change it. Without /SHARE a file opened to read can be
 read by others, and one opened to write is the opener's alone; an open
 that conflicts fails with RMS-E-FLK.
/ERROR

    /ERROR=label

 Goes to label if the file can't be opened, with no message.
2 Example
 $ OPEN/WRITE OUT REPORT.TXT
 $ WRITE OUT "first line"
 $ CLOSE OUT
1 PIPE
 Runs commands joined by pipes, one after the other, or depending on how
 the one before went.

 Format

   PIPE command [| command ...] [; && || ...]
2 Parameters
command

 A command, or several in parentheses (a subshell), with any of:

   < file-spec     input from the file
   > file-spec     output to a new version of the file
   2> file-spec    SYS$ERROR to a new version of the file

 The redirections need a blank in front.
2 Joining
 a | b      b reads what a writes; each runs in a DCL subprocess, and
            reads SYS$PIPE (or SYS$INPUT) as its input
 a ; b      a, then b
 a && b     b only if a worked
 a || b     b only if a failed

 A command alone, with no pipe and no redirection, runs in this process,
 so PIPE A = 1 ; SHOW SYMBOL A sets A here.
2 Status
 After a pipeline $STATUS has the last command's status, marked as shown.
 If the last part didn't run (after && or ||), it is 0.
2 Examples
 1.$ PIPE TYPE NOTES.TXT | SEARCH SYS$PIPE TODO

 2.$ PIPE (WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "a" ; WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "b") | SEARCH SYS$PIPE A

 3.$ PIPE SHOW LOGICAL SYS$LOGIN > LOGIN.TXT
1 PURGE
 Deletes all but the newest versions of files.

 Format

   PURGE [file-spec[,...]]
2 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 The files to purge; the default is *.* in the current default
 directory.
2 Qualifiers
/KEEP

    /KEEP=n

 Keeps the n newest versions (the default is 1).
/LOG
 Shows each version deleted, %PURGE-I-FILPURG, and the total.
/EXCLUDE

    /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't skip anything yet.
1 READ
 Reads a record from a file opened with OPEN, or a line from the
 terminal or a pipe, into a local symbol.

 Format

   READ logical-name symbol-name
2 Parameters
logical-name

 The name given to OPEN, or SYS$INPUT, SYS$COMMAND, SYS$PIPE or TT for
 DCL's input.

symbol-name

 The local symbol for the record.
2 Qualifiers
/END_OF_FILE

    /END_OF_FILE=label

 Goes to label at the end of the file. Without it, the end of the file
 is an error, %RMS-E-EOF.
/PROMPT

    /PROMPT=string

 The prompt when reading the terminal.
/ERROR

    /ERROR=label

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't take it yet.
/KEY

    /KEY=string

 Reads the record whose key matches the string, in the key /INDEX names
 (the primary key by default); a string shorter than the key matches its
 leading part. Later READs go on in that key's order. A relative file's
 key is the record number as 4 bytes.
/INDEX

    /INDEX=n

 The key /KEY reads by: 0 is the primary key, 1 the first alternate.
/MATCH

    /MATCH=option

 How /KEY matches: EQ (the default), GE, GT, LE or LT, the record whose
 key is equal to the string, at or after it, after it, at or before it,
 or before it.
/DELETE
 Deletes the record once it is read.
/NOLOCK
 Reads the record without locking it, even when another stream has it
 locked. Without /NOLOCK, in a file others may change, the record stays
 locked against other streams until this one's next operation.
/TIME_OUT

    /TIME_OUT=n

 Accepted; not used yet.
2 Example
 $ OPEN IN NOTES.TXT
 $LOOP:
 $ READ/END_OF_FILE=DONE IN LINE
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT LINE
 $ GOTO LOOP
 $DONE:
 $ CLOSE IN
1 RETURN
 Goes back from a GOSUB to the command after it.

 Format

   RETURN [status]
2 Parameters
status

 An expression for $STATUS; a failure is shown. RETURN with no GOSUB to
 go back to does nothing, as on VMS.
1 SEARCH
 Searches files for lines that hold one or more strings.

 Format

   SEARCH file-spec[,...] search-string[,...]
2 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 The files to search; wildcards allowed. SYS$PIPE and SYS$INPUT are the
 command's input.

search-string[,...]

 What to look for. DCL upcases a string that isn't in quotation marks.
2 Qualifiers
/EXACT
 Matches case exactly; by default case doesn't matter.
/HEADING

    /HEADING (default)
    /NOHEADING

 When several files are searched, shows each file's name, between lines
 of asterisks, before its matches.
/LOG
 Shows each file searched and how many lines matched.
/MATCH

    /MATCH=keyword

 OR (the default): a line with any of the strings. AND: with all of
 them. NAND: without all of them. NOR: with none.
/NUMBERS
 Shows each line's number.
/WINDOW

    /WINDOW[=n]
    /WINDOW=(before,after)

 Shows lines around each match: n lines in all, or before and after.
/STATISTICS
 Accepted; no statistics are shown yet.
/OUTPUT

    /OUTPUT=file-spec

 Accepted; the lines go to SYS$OUTPUT.
/EXCLUDE

    /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't skip anything yet.
2 Examples
 1.$ SEARCH *.COM "SET VERIFY"

 2.$ SEARCH/NUMBERS/WINDOW=3 NOTES.TXT TODO
1 SET
 Changes something about the DCL session.

 Format

   SET option
2 COMMAND
 Adds the verbs a CLD file defines to this process's command tables, or
 writes the tables for a program.

 Format

   SET COMMAND [file-spec[,...]]
3 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 The CLD files; the default type is .CLD. A verb's IMAGE may be a VMS
 file spec, a logical name, or a Unix program, which gets the command's
 parameters as its argv.
3 Qualifiers
/DELETE

    /DELETE=(verb[,...])

 Takes verbs out of this process's tables.
/OBJECT

    /OBJECT[=file-spec]

 Writes the tables as C source for a program to pass to cli$dcl_parse,
 instead of changing this process's tables. The default file is the
 first CLD's name with type .C (vmsport cdu does the same from a Unix
 shell).
3 Example
 $ SET COMMAND [.TOOLS]GREET
 $ GREET/COUNT=3 WORLD
2 DEFAULT
 Changes the default directory, the one file specs use when they don't
 name a directory.

 Format

   SET DEFAULT [device:][directory]
3 Parameters
directory

 A device, a directory or both. [.SUB] and [-] are relative to the
 current default. vmsport, like VMS, doesn't check that it exists.
3 Example
 $ SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN:
 $ SET DEFAULT [.PROJECTS.VMSPORT]
2 MESSAGE
 Adds a message file, or chooses which parts of messages show.

 Format

   SET MESSAGE [file-spec]
3 Parameters
file-spec

 A message file: .MSG source, or one compiled by vmsport. Its messages
 come before the system's.
3 Qualifiers
/FACILITY

    /FACILITY (default)
    /NOFACILITY

 Shows the facility name, %FACILITY-S-IDENT.
/IDENTIFICATION

    /IDENTIFICATION (default)
    /NOIDENTIFICATION

 Shows the message's name.
/SEVERITY

    /SEVERITY (default)
    /NOSEVERITY

 Shows the severity letter.
/TEXT

    /TEXT (default)
    /NOTEXT

 Shows the message text.
/DELETE
 Accepted; message files can't be taken out yet.
2 ON
 SET ON turns on the ON checks for this procedure level (the default);
 SET NOON turns them off, so failing commands don't stop the procedure.

 Format

   SET [NO]ON
2 SYMBOL
 Accepted for compatibility; symbol scoping is not there yet.

 Format

   SET SYMBOL
3 Qualifiers
/SCOPE

    /SCOPE=(keyword[,...])

 Accepted; does nothing yet.
/VERB
 Accepted; does nothing yet.
/GENERAL
 Accepted; does nothing yet.
2 VERIFY
 SET VERIFY shows each line of a procedure before it runs (see
 Command_Procedures, Verify); SET NOVERIFY stops it.

 Format

   SET [NO]VERIFY
1 SHOW
 Shows something about the DCL session.

 Format

   SHOW option
2 DEFAULT
 Shows the default device and directory.

 Format

   SHOW DEFAULT
2 LOGICAL
 Shows logical names and their values, in each table that has them;
 values that are logical names themselves are translated in turn.

 Format

   SHOW LOGICAL [logical-name[,...]]
3 Parameters
logical-name[,...]

 The names to show; without them, all names in LNM$DCL_LOGICAL. A name
 with no value gives %SHOW-S-NOTRAN.
3 Qualifiers
/PROCESS
 Only the process table.
/JOB
 Only the job table.
/GROUP
 Only the group table.
/SYSTEM
 Only the system table.
/TABLE

    /TABLE=(name[,...])

 Only these tables.
/FULL
 Shows access modes, attributes (concealed, terminal) and the tables
 themselves too.
/ALL
 All the tables in LNM$DCL_LOGICAL (the default).
3 Example
 $ SHOW LOGICAL SYS$LOGIN
2 STATUS
 Shows the time. Process statistics are not there yet.

 Format

   SHOW STATUS
2 SYMBOL
 Shows a symbol's value: integers in decimal, hex and octal, strings in
 quotation marks; = for a local, == for a global.

 Format

   SHOW SYMBOL [symbol-name]
3 Parameters
symbol-name

 The symbol; the local one if there is one, else the global. $STATUS and
 $SEVERITY show too. Without a name, the current level's locals and the
 globals.
3 Qualifiers
/LOCAL
 Only the current level's local symbols.
/GLOBAL
 Only the global symbols.
/ALL
 Every symbol (with /LOCAL or /GLOBAL, all of those).
/LOG
 Accepted; does nothing.
2 TIME
 Shows the date and time.

 Format

   SHOW TIME
2 TRANSLATION
 Shows a logical name's value, as SHOW LOGICAL does for one name.

 Format

   SHOW TRANSLATION logical-name
3 Parameters
logical-name

 The name to translate.
3 Qualifiers
/TABLE

    /TABLE=name

 The table to look in; the default is LNM$DCL_LOGICAL.
1 Specify
 A file spec names a file, VMS style:

   node::device:[directory]name.type;version

 Each part may be left out; what is missing comes from defaults, the
 default directory (SET DEFAULT) and logical names. DCL upcases specs it
 isn't given in quotation marks; names are found whatever their case.
2 Host_Files
 HOST: is the host's /: HOST:[Users.me.notes]TODO.TXT is the file
 /Users/me/notes/TODO.TXT. A directory shows as NAME.DIR;1.

 Versions are part of the host name: TODO.TXT;3 is the file
 "TODO.TXT;3". A Unix file without ;n is version 1. New files are given
 the next version, and Unix tools see the versions as they are.

 Record attributes (fixed, variable, stream...) are kept in the file's
 vms.fab extended attribute; a file without it is stream_LF text.

 vmsport path SPEC prints the host path of a spec; vmsport spec PATH the
 spec of a host path.
2 Directories
 [A.B] is a directory from the top of the device, [.A] one below the
 default, [-] the one above it, [] the default itself, [000000] the top.
 [...] in a wildcard search is a directory and all below it; * and % in
 a directory name match subdirectories.
2 Versions
 ;n is version n, ;0 or no version the newest, ;-1 the one before it,
 ;* all of them (in searches).
2 Wildcards
 * matches any characters, % one character, in names, types and
 directory names; ;* matches all versions.
2 Extended_Names
 Names may hold characters VMS escapes with ^ (ODS-5): ^. a dot in a
 name, ^_ or "^ " a blank, ^XX a character in hex, ^Uxxxx a Unicode
 one, and ^ before other punctuation. Extra dots belong to the name: the
 last dot starts the type, so archive.tar.gz is name "archive^.tar",
 type "gz".
2 Logical_Names
 A device may be a logical name: SYS$LOGIN:NOTES.TXT. A name alone may
 be a logical name for a whole spec. See Logical_Names.
1 SPAWN
 Runs a command, or a session of commands, in a DCL subprocess. The
 subprocess gets this process's symbols, process logical names and
 default directory; what it changes doesn't come back.

 Format

   SPAWN [command]
2 Parameters
command

 The command to run. Without it the subprocess reads commands from the
 terminal until LOGOUT.
2 Qualifiers
/WAIT

    /WAIT (default)
    /NOWAIT

 /NOWAIT goes on at once, while the subprocess runs.
/LOG

    /LOG (default)
    /NOLOG

 Shows %DCL-S-SPAWNED and %DCL-S-ATTACHED when the subprocess starts and
 %DCL-S-RETURNED when it ends.
/SYMBOL

    /SYMBOL (default)
    /NOSYMBOL

 Gives the subprocess this process's symbols.
/LOGICAL_NAMES

    /LOGICAL_NAMES (default)
    /NOLOGICAL_NAMES

 Gives the subprocess this process's process logical names.
/OUTPUT

    /OUTPUT=file-spec

 The subprocess's SYS$OUTPUT, a new version of the file.
/INPUT

    /INPUT=file-spec

 The subprocess's input.
/PROCESS

    /PROCESS=name

 The subprocess's name; the default is this process's name followed by
 _n.
/NOTIFY
 Accepted; no notice is given.
/CARRIAGE_CONTROL
 Accepted; does nothing.
/KEYPAD
 Accepted; does nothing.
2 Status
 $STATUS has the subprocess's last status; a failure shows again in the
 parent.
2 Examples
 1.$ SPAWN DIRECTORY

 2.$ SPAWN/NOWAIT/OUTPUT=BUILD.LOG @BUILD
1 STOP
 Ends every procedure level and goes back to the terminal, with
 SS$_ABORT in $STATUS.

 Format

   STOP
1 SUBROUTINE
 Starts a subroutine, the target of CALL: a block of commands from
 label: SUBROUTINE to ENDSUBROUTINE. A procedure that reaches it without
 CALL steps over it.

 Format

   label: SUBROUTINE
       commands
   ENDSUBROUTINE
2 ENDSUBROUTINE
 Ends the block; reaching it returns from the CALL with %X00030001.
1 Symbol_Assign
 Symbols are DCL's variables: names for integers and strings.

 Format

   symbol = expression           local
   symbol == expression          global
   symbol := string              local, string as typed
   symbol :== string             global, string as typed
   symbol[start,length] := string
   symbol[bit,size] = expression
2 String_Assignment
 := and :== take the rest of the line as a string: upcased and squeezed
 to single blanks outside quotation marks; the quotation marks go.
2 Substrings
 symbol[start,length] := string puts string into those characters (from
 0), padding with blanks.
2 Bit_Fields
 symbol[bit,size] = expression puts the value into those bits of an
 integer (from bit 0, at most 32 bits in all).
2 Abbreviations
 A * in the name says how short it may be: ABBREV*IATION = 1 defines
 ABBREV, ABBREVI, ... ABBREVIATION.
2 Local_And_Global
 Locals belong to the procedure level (procedure or CALL) that defines
 them, and inner levels see them; globals are seen everywhere. A local
 hides a global of the same name.
2 Substitution
 'symbol' in a command line is replaced by the symbol's value (the
 closing apostrophe is optional), and so is 'F$FUNCTION(...)'. Inside
 quotation marks, write ''symbol'. An undefined symbol gives "". In an
 expression, &symbol is replaced by its value before the expression is
 read.
2 Verbs
 A symbol at the start of a command stands for its value: DIR :==
 DIRECTORY/SIZE makes DIR a command. One whose value starts with $ is a
 foreign command (see Foreign_Commands).
1 THEN
 Starts the part of an IF block that runs when the condition is true.
 See IF.

 Format

   THEN
1 TYPE
 Shows files on SYS$OUTPUT, their records as lines, with the carriage
 control turned into new lines, blank lines and form feeds.

 Format

   TYPE file-spec[,...]
2 Parameters
file-spec[,...]

 The files to show; wildcards allowed. SYS$INPUT and SYS$PIPE are the
 command's input. When several files are shown, each after the first
 (and the first, for wildcards) starts with its name.
2 Qualifiers
/PAGE
 Accepted; the files go out without stopping.
/OUTPUT

    /OUTPUT=file-spec

 Accepted; the files go to SYS$OUTPUT.
/EXCLUDE

    /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't skip anything yet.
1 Unix
 vmsport is DCL and VMS-style programs on the host; all of it works from
 a Unix shell too.
2 dcl
 dcl                      an interactive session (LOGIN.COM first)
 dcl -c 'COMMAND' ...     the commands, then exit 0 if the last worked
 dcl FILE.COM [P1 ...]    @FILE.COM

 At the terminal dcl recalls commands with the arrow keys (or Ctrl/B);
 Ctrl/Z ends the session.
2 Utilities
 directory, type, copy, delete, purge, search and help are programs:
 DCL runs them for their verbs, and from a shell they take the same
 command, as arguments:

   directory /size '[.src]'
   search '*.rs' unwrap /numbers

 Quote what the shell would expand.
2 Tools
 vmsport path SPEC        the host path of a VMS file spec
 vmsport spec PATH        the VMS spec of a host path
 vmsport cdu FILE.CLD     a CLD as C source for cli$dcl_parse
 lnm ...                  job logical names (see Logical_Names)
2 Programs
 Programs of your own get their commands from a CLD, like the
 utilities. In Rust, libvms::cli has dcl_parse, present, get_value,
 get_symbol, set_symbol and exit. In C, include $VMSPORT/include
 (cli$routines.h, lib$routines.h, starlet.h, descrip.h ...) and link with
 -lvms for cli$present, cli$get_value, cli$dcl_parse, lib$get_foreign,
 lib$set_symbol, lib$signal, sys$exit and friends. Leave with sys$exit
 so DCL gets the whole status. See SET COMMAND and docs/design/m2.md.
2 Daemon
 vmsportd keeps the shared logical name tables. Its socket is in
 $VMSPORT_RUN, or ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/vmsport-UID; keep that path short
 (about 100 bytes at most).
1 WRITE
 Writes a record to a file opened with OPEN, or a line to SYS$OUTPUT.

 Format

   WRITE logical-name expression[,...]
2 Parameters
logical-name

 SYS$OUTPUT, SYS$ERROR or TT, or the name given to OPEN.

expression[,...]

 The values to write, joined with nothing between them; integers are
 written in decimal.
2 Qualifiers
/SYMBOL
 Accepted; vmsport has no 255-character limit to work around.
/UPDATE
 Accepted; updating records in place comes with M3.
/ERROR

    /ERROR=label

 Accepted; vmsport doesn't take it yet.
2 Example
 $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Found ", COUNT, " file", F$FAO("!%S", COUNT)
